SELECT 'core-2026-09-v4','relative-clauses','87b630c771d79eac83e3be02f06e226cf9455e201ca4d4e9ebd574542396373d',payload || '{"slug":"relative-clauses","title":"Defining and non-defining relative clauses","level":"B1","order":49,"summary":"Identify a person or thing precisely, or add extra information without changing who you mean.","guidance":"Read the form and meaning together. Study the contrasts, then explain a worked example in your own words before starting a fresh practice session.","learningGoals":["Defining information","Extra information","Possession and place","When omission is possible"],"rules":[{"title":"Defining information","explanation":"A defining relative clause tells us which person or thing is meant and usually has no surrounding commas. Who refers to people; which refers to things; that can often refer to either in defining clauses. The relative pronoun can be subject or object within its clause.","pattern":"The person **who called** left a message.","commonMistakes":["Do not mark that as wrong where it is a valid defining relative alternative to who or which."],"examples":["Choose a person-relative pronoun: “The assistant **who** prepares the visitor guide is here.”","Choose a person-relative pronoun: “The assistant **who** restores the old portrait is here.”"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Extra information","explanation":"A non-defining relative clause adds information about an already identified referent and is set off with commas. Use who or which, not that. The relative pronoun is not omitted in this type of clause.","pattern":"My bicycle, **which is red**, is outside.","commonMistakes":["Leaving out the commas may change whether the information identifies the referent or merely adds a detail."],"examples":["Add extra information: “Our assistant, **who** prepares the visitor guide every week, is away.”","Add extra information: “Our assistant, **who** restores the old portrait every week, is away.”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Possession and place","explanation":"Whose marks a possessive relationship and comes before a noun. Where introduces a relative clause about a place when that place has an adverbial role. Compare the place where we met with the place which we visited: visited takes a direct object.","pattern":"The artist **whose work** won is here.","commonMistakes":["Where is not a universal substitute for which after a place noun; the role inside the clause matters."],"examples":["Express possession: “The assistant **whose** job is to prepare the visitor guide has arrived.”","Express possession: “The assistant **whose** job is to restore the old portrait has arrived.”"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"When omission is possible","explanation":"In a defining clause, an object relative pronoun can often be omitted. A subject relative pronoun cannot normally be omitted. Non-defining clauses keep the pronoun regardless of its role.","pattern":"The book **(that) I read** was short. / The book **that won** was long.","commonMistakes":["The person works here without a relative pronoun is a main clause, not a defining clause attached to another noun phrase."],"examples":["In “The task that we chose was to prepare the visitor guide”, what is true about “that”? → **It can be omitted because it is the object in a defining clause.**","In “The task that we chose was to restore the old portrait”, what is true about “that”? → **It can be omitted because it is the object in a defining clause.**"],"skill":"application"}],"tips":[],"source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-relative","bc-nondefining"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"chapter":{"introduction":["Identify a person or thing precisely, or add extra information without changing who you mean.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Defining information","explanation":"A defining relative clause tells us which person or thing is meant and usually has no surrounding commas. Who refers to people; which refers to things; that can often refer to either in defining clauses. The relative pronoun can be subject or object within its clause.","pattern":"The person **who called** left a message.","commonMistakes":["Do not mark that as wrong where it is a valid defining relative alternative to who or which."],"examples":["Choose a person-relative pronoun: “The assistant **who** prepares the visitor guide is here.”","Choose a person-relative pronoun: “The assistant **who** restores the old portrait is here.”"],"skill":"form","id":"relative-clauses-foundation-1","domain":"relatives","referenceIds":["bc-relative","bc-nondefining"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v3"},{"title":"Extra information","explanation":"A non-defining relative clause adds information about an already identified referent and is set off with commas. Use who or which, not that. The relative pronoun is not omitted in this type of clause.","pattern":"My bicycle, **which is red**, is outside.","commonMistakes":["Leaving out the commas may change whether the information identifies the referent or merely adds a detail."],"examples":["Add extra information: “Our assistant, **who** prepares the visitor guide every week, is away.”","Add extra information: “Our assistant, **who** restores the old portrait every week, is away.”"],"skill":"meaning","id":"relative-clauses-foundation-2","domain":"relatives","referenceIds":["bc-relative","bc-nondefining"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v3"},{"title":"Possession and place","explanation":"Whose marks a possessive relationship and comes before a noun. Where introduces a relative clause about a place when that place has an adverbial role. Compare the place where we met with the place which we visited: visited takes a direct object.","pattern":"The artist **whose work** won is here.","commonMistakes":["Where is not a universal substitute for which after a place noun; the role inside the clause matters."],"examples":["Express possession: “The assistant **whose** job is to prepare the visitor guide has arrived.”","Express possession: “The assistant **whose** job is to restore the old portrait has arrived.”"],"skill":"contrast","id":"relative-clauses-foundation-3","domain":"relatives","referenceIds":["bc-relative","bc-nondefining"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v3"},{"title":"When omission is possible","explanation":"In a defining clause, an object relative pronoun can often be omitted. A subject relative pronoun cannot normally be omitted. Non-defining clauses keep the pronoun regardless of its role.","pattern":"The book **(that) I read** was short. / The book **that won** was long.","commonMistakes":["The person works here without a relative pronoun is a main clause, not a defining clause attached to another noun phrase."],"examples":["In “The task that we chose was to prepare the visitor guide”, what is true about “that”? → **It can be omitted because it is the object in a defining clause.**","In “The task that we chose was to restore the old portrait”, what is true about “that”? → **It can be omitted because it is the object in a defining clause.**"],"skill":"application","id":"relative-clauses-foundation-4","domain":"relatives","referenceIds":["bc-relative","bc-nondefining"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v3"},{"domain":"relatives","id":"relative-gap-functions","title":"Relative pronouns as subjects, objects and complements","explanation":"A relative construction connects a noun or other antecedent to a missing role inside a clause. The relative expression may supply the subject, object, possessive determiner or complement of a preposition. Locate the missing role before choosing who, whom, which, whose, that or omission. A noun’s meaning alone is insufficient.","pattern":"the city **which we visited** / the city **where we stayed**","examples":["The person **whom we invited** declined.","The tool **with which she worked** was old."],"commonMistakes":["Do not repeat the object when the relative already represents it: the book that I read, not the book that I read it in the ordinary standard construction."],"referenceIds":["cambridge-comprehensive-scope"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v4"},{"domain":"relatives","id":"whole-clause-relative","title":"Relative clauses referring to a whole situation","explanation":"A supplementary which-clause can comment on an entire preceding proposition rather than on one noun. Commas signal that it is additional information. The interpretation comes from the meaning of the comment and the preceding clause. Keep the antecedent clear when the clause contains several possible nouns and events.","pattern":"She missed the train, **which delayed the trip**.","examples":["He apologised, **which surprised everyone**.","The road was closed, **which meant a long detour**."],"commonMistakes":["That does not normally introduce this supplementary whole-clause relative. If which could refer to a noun or event ambiguously, split the message into two sentences."],"referenceIds":["cambridge-comprehensive-scope"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v4"},{"domain":"relatives","id":"fused-free-relatives","title":"What, whoever and fused relative constructions","explanation":"A fused or free relative combines the role of a noun-like expression with a relative clause, without a separate head noun. What can mean the thing that; whoever can mean any person who. These expressions can function as subjects or objects. Some -ever clauses instead express a concessive condition.","pattern":"Keep **what you need**.","examples":["**Whoever arrives first** can choose a seat.","I liked **what she suggested**."],"commonMistakes":["Do not add a separate antecedent before relative what in ordinary standard English: the thing that I need or what I need, not the thing what I need."],"referenceIds":["cambridge-comprehensive-scope"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v4"},{"domain":"relatives","id":"relative-quantifiers-and-whereby","title":"Quantified relatives, whose for things and whereby","explanation":"A supplementary relative can contain a quantifier plus of whom or of which. Whose can refer to things as well as people when it marks a possessive relationship. Whereby is a formal relative connector meaning by which means. These patterns let a writer attach a precise relationship to an identified set or process.","pattern":"ten applicants, **three of whom** were selected","examples":["We visited a village **whose roads were flooded**.","They introduced a system **whereby members could vote online**."],"commonMistakes":["Keep the quantifier’s agreement clear: one of whom was selected but three of whom were selected. Do not substitute who for of whom after this quantifier pattern."],"referenceIds":["cambridge-comprehensive-scope"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v4"},{"domain":"relatives","id":"long-distance-dependencies","title":"Long-distance questions and relative links","explanation":"A question or relative expression can correspond to a role inside an embedded clause: the person who I think called. The grammatical form depends on that embedded role. Such dependencies have limits; not every expression can be moved out of every clause or noun phrase. Reword a construction when a distant link becomes hard to follow.","pattern":"Who do you think **___ called**? (The gap marks the subject’s position.)","examples":["This is the book **that I said I needed**.","Who do you believe **will win**?"],"commonMistakes":["In Who do you think called?, who is the embedded subject. Its distance from called does not turn it into an object requiring whom."],"referenceIds":["cambridge-comprehensive-scope"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v4"}],"recap":["The person **who called** left a message.","My bicycle, **which is red**, is outside.","The artist **whose work** won is here.","The book **(that) I read** was short. / The book **that won** was long."],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v4"},"stage":"5 · Complex sentences","prerequisites":["passive-voice"],"readingReferenceIds":["bc-relative","bc-nondefining","cambridge-comprehensive-scope"]}'::jsonb FROM grammar.content_catalog_topics WHERE version='core-2026-09-v3' AND slug='relative-clauses'
